WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.680
After six seasons, years of rich historical
drama and a great deal of ratings success,

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.180
what exactly made History decide to axe its
flagship show?

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.220
These are the real reasons why Vikings is
finally coming to an end.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.220
Growing up on his parents' farm in Australia,
Travis Fimmel was a million miles away from

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:22.460
the bright lights of Hollywood, but a chance
encounter with a modeling scout changed the

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.000
course of his life forever.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:28.140
After a stint working in London, he decided
to try his luck Stateside, and according to

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:34.039
the Sydney Morning Herald, was offered a contract
instantly after, quote, "swaggering barefoot

00:00:34.039 --> 00:00:35.440
into LA Models."

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.230
He quickly became the face of Calvin Klein,
but while he made money posing in his underwear,

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.920
he was also fine-tuning his acting skills.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.620
Fimmel had a number of roles in Australia
prior to Vikings, but it was playing the show's

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.720
enigmatic lead that brought him international
recognition.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.080
His intense and often sympathetic portrayal
of farmer-turned-Viking leader Ragnar Lothbrok

00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:01.500
was the true heart of the show, so when the
character was killed off in season 4, Vikings

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.320
entered uncharted territory.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.540
According to Forbes writer Erik Kain, it was
a gamble that ultimately didn't pay off.

00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:10.640
After the show’s cancellation, he wrote:

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.860
"Thank goodness.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:15.860
The show has been lost at sea for a long time
now, and probably should have wrapped with

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.430
the death of Ragnar and departure of lead,
Travis Fimmel."

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:24.020
When Ragnar met his end in season 4, viewers
were supposed to get on board with the idea

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:26.800
of his sons heading to England to avenge his
death.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:31.320
We were introduced to his first-born son,
Bjorn, way back in the first season, but we

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.940
didn't really get to know the rest of Ragnar's
brood until after the season 4 time jump.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.520
Vikings creator Michael Hirst told Variety:

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.420
"It was very important to bring on Ragnar's
sons.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.880
I've invested a lot of time, hope and expectation
in these sons.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.360
It's very important that the audience invest
in these new characters."

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:51.720
Unfortunately for Hirst, this never really
happened.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:56.479
Despite his best efforts, audiences just didn't
seem as interested in watching the antics

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.900
of Ragnar's offspring, even when they were
hell-bent on avenging the man himself.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.220
After new of the show’s end broke, Forbes
reported:

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:08.740
"Ragnar's story was over a long time ago and
none of the stories that have come since have

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.820
resonated as much with viewers.”

00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:14.480
TV Equals placed the blame directly at the
feet of Ragnar's sons.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.240
They wrote:

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.190
"The death of Ragnar Lothbrok has left a void
that his sons have not yet been able to fill…

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.580
most of Ragnar's sons are kind of boring."

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:27.440
History ordered a sixth season of Vikings
before season 5 had even begun.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:32.200
At the time, nobody knew that the sixth season
would be the last, but the decision to cancel

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.000
the show seems to have been influenced (at
least in part) by a decline in viewership.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:42.030
Following the season 5 premiere, CarterMatt
tried to soften the blow by suggesting that

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:47.159
it's "hard for any cable series to amass large
numbers these days," but the facts are still

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.220
hard to ignore.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:49.240
The outlet wrote:

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:54.320
"The bad news is that these ratings are down
fairly substantially from the 0.8 rating that

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:58.680
the show generated for its season 4 premiere
back in February 2016.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.920
It's also down about 9 percent in total viewers.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:07.500
Versus the average season 4 rating, the Vikings
premiere is down about 13 percent in the demo

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.340
and 4 percent in the viewers."

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:14.340
This wasn't a new trend, either: the previous
season also saw a significant drop.

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:20.040
According to TV Series Finale, season 4 of
Vikings was down by a whopping "28 percent

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.840
in the demo and 44 percent in viewers."

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.280
History renewed anyway, but the network must
have recognized that the show was approaching

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:27.780
its end.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:32.460
One surefire way of getting waning viewers
to stick with a show is to announce that the

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:37.360
next season will be the last, and it seems
the network wanted to wrap Vikings up before

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:38.860
it saw a mass exodus.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:44.780
Gustaf Skarsgård's Floki has been a fan favorite
since day one, but the character underwent

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:46.900
wholesale changes in season 5.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:52.120
He sails with Ragnar's sons as part of the
Great Heathen Army, but once they avenge him,

00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:54.360
Floki becomes little more than a spare part.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.640
"I feel trapped in all this happiness.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:00.180
I feel trapped, Helga."

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:04.879
He then takes to the seas alone and puts himself
at the mercy of the Gods, who lead him to

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.260
what he believes to be Asgard, but we know
today as Iceland.

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.920
Floki decides to bring a handful of settlers
to this new, wild land, hoping to build a

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:17.140
community free of the violence that had become
an everyday part of Viking life.

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:22.240
Despite Floki's attempts at keeping the peace,
petty arguments turn into blood feuds and

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:23.660
the settlement struggles.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.790
It ought to have run across a few episodes
at best, but the Iceland plot was still ongoing

00:04:28.790 --> 00:04:32.640
at the end of season 5, testing the patience
of many viewers.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.260
One ticked off Redditor wrote:

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:38.100
"I can't believe I gave Hirst the benefit
of the doubt with this storyline.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.740
The drama between Eyvind and Floki has been
forced from the start, and they've made absolutely

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.980
no effort to get us to care about any of the
new characters."

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:51.080
Floki's voyage to Iceland may have been inspired
by true events, but sadly it made for some

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:52.700
pretty boring television.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:57.600
When Travis Fimmel departed Vikings, Katheryn
Winnick became the new anchor of the show.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:03.160
Ragnar's first wife Lagertha has always been
loved by fans, so this seemed a smart move.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.380
But while Winnick made a name for herself
playing Lagertha, like Fimmel, it was only

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.940
a matter of time before she too moved on to
bigger things.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.200
Vikings may have sailed a little off course
in season 5, but Winnick remains in demand.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:21.360
The Dark Tower was a letdown for her and Polar
didn't exactly go to plan, but Winnick is

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:27.600
set to return to TV with a brand new Netflix
show in 2019, Wu Assassins, a martial arts

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.440
crime series set in San Francisco's Chinatown.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:35.620
Winnick's in-show son Alexander Ludwig is
also a hot property thanks to his portrayal

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.020
of Bjorn Ironside.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:41.500
Ludwig had previously appeared in the Hunger
Games franchise, but Vikings catapulted him

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:42.960
to new levels of fame.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:44.600
He told Channel 24:

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.220
"I'd never experienced anything like that.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:51.400
It's amazing how much this show has really
affected people in all walks of life globally."

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.360
It’s not a stretch to imagine that Ludwig
must be in as much demand as Winnick and Fimmel.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:02.520
When the first episode of The Last Kingdom
aired in 2015, some had already dismissed

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:04.000
it as a Vikings knockoff.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.480
By the time the first season was done, however,
many minds had been changed.

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:11.020
In its review for the show, The AV Club wrote:

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.940
"BBC America's sprawling, arresting eight-part
historical miniseries The Last Kingdom proves

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.700
that there's room enough on television for
more than one Viking invasion.”

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:25.100
The show (which is based on the Saxon Stories
novels by Bernard Cornwell) was praised by

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:29.460
critics across the board, creating an instant
rivalry with Vikings.

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.200
Of course, Vikings and The Last Kingdom aren't
the only two shows set during that tumultuous

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.920
period in European history.

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:40.820
When Erik Kain expressed relief over Vikings
ending in his Forbes piece, he pointed out

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:43.080
that the market was becoming highly saturated.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:44.040
Kain wrote:

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:47.360
"We have The Last Kingdom airing still on
Netflix after all.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:49.700
Oh, and there's also the wonderful Norsemen.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:52.140
Viking shows are becoming like zombie shows."

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.000
Norsemen is a comedy show, so it wasn’t
ever really a direct threat to Vikings, but

00:06:57.000 --> 00:07:01.620
its success further proves that History isn't
the only network capable of creating a compelling

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.780
show about the Viking way of life.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:08.640
The Viking trend has also hit the big screen
with Northmen: A Viking Saga arriving hot

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:13.800
on the heels of Vikings in 2014, and Norse
culture remains prevalent in Hollywood today

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.480
thanks to the How to Train Your Dragon franchise.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.840
Viewers have lots of different places to go
should they want a Viking fix, and Vikings

00:07:20.840 --> 00:07:22.700
itself has suffered for that fact.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.320
Despite everything, many Vikings fans still
believe the show is being sent to Valhalla

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.260
far too soon.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:33.780
Disgruntled viewers took to Twitter to vent
when the news broke, comedian Kathleen Madigan

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:34.480
among them.

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:35.240
She tweeted:

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.000
"Vikings [is] being canceled after season
6.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.640
I'll just go ahead and prepare my own viking
funeral in solidarity.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:42.480
Sad news."

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:46.440
And while some fans might point out that the
show has already covered everything historians

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:51.300
know about Ragnar and his sons, that doesn't
necessarily mean Vikings has to end.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:56.360
Hirst’s other show The Tudors was notorious
for being historically inaccurate, but even

00:07:56.370 --> 00:08:00.610
leading historians admitted that the things
Hirst added worked well for TV.

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.920
Dr. Tracy Borman told The Telegraph:

00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:07.460
"Yes, the scriptwriters may have taken liberties
with the facts, but they have also succeeded

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:11.080
in re-creating the drama and atmosphere of
Henry VIII's court."

00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:15.900
Vikings star Alexander Ludwig has praised
Hirst's willingness to take a "creative liberty"

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:20.040
with the show, which he could well have continued
to do for many more seasons.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:24.960
The decline in ratings was undoubtedly on
the mind of History executives when they agreed

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.470
to bring Vikings to an end, but Michael Hirst
didn't mention any of that when he sat down

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.880
with Variety in January 2019.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:37.060
Officially, the interview was about the season
5 finale, but the show's cancellation inevitably

00:08:37.060 --> 00:08:37.680
came up.

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:42.120
Pressed for details, Hirst claimed that six
seasons was actually enough time for him to

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.780
tell the story he'd wanted to tell from the
very beginning.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:45.680
He said:

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:49.960
"What I was trying to do was write the saga
of Ragnar Lothbrok and his sons.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:54.500
After six seasons and 89 episodes, that's
what I felt, finally, I'd done…

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:57.500
I had no reason to want to continue it beyond
that."

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.300
"Don’t waste your time looking back.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.020
You’re not going that way."

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:05.500
Hirst was quick to remind Variety readers
that the funeral pyre hadn't been lit just

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:06.000
yet.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:10.320
In an effort to lift spirits among the wounded
fans, the showrunner started hyping up the

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.920
sixth and final season.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:12.930
He told the publication:

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:18.640
"We visit three new worlds in those episodes,
we go through great tragedies and great heroism,

00:09:18.650 --> 00:09:22.720
and if you have tears to weep be prepared
to weep them as you watch season 6.

00:09:22.720 --> 00:09:24.260
I'm very proud of the show.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:26.440
I did what I wanted to do, that's the truth."

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:32.450
Losing Vikings is a huge blow for the show's
many dedicated fans, but it was softened by

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:35.140
news that a spinoff series is in the cards.

00:09:35.410 --> 00:09:39.220
When Variety asked Hirst what he had planned
for this new show, the writer and producer

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.130
said that he considers it a "continuation
series."

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:43.200
"We were defeated!"

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:44.840
"We live to fight another day."

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:48.920
Hirst went on to explain that he had developed
an attachment to the characters he brought

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:53.800
to life in Vikings, which is why he wanted
to be involved in any new show set in that

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:54.300
world.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:55.480
Hirst said:

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.640
"I have to say that they're not characters
to me.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:01.190
I write so much about them, I've lived with
them for seven years.

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:02.200
They're real people to me.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.500
So I care, and have been caring, about them
for a long time.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:08.800
I want to guide them through whatever storylines
they have."

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:13.400
So does this mean we'll see more of our favorite
Vikings characters pop up in this continuation

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:13.900
series?

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.100
And what does 'continuation series' even mean?

00:10:16.420 --> 00:10:17.180
Hirst explained:

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:21.160
"Continuation means that if we make it, it
will be connected to my saga.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.340
It may not involve necessarily the same characters.

00:10:23.500 --> 00:10:28.540
I can't go into more details than that, but
for the fans of the show it will be a continued

00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:30.820
enjoyment of the Vikings experience."

